WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.873
Welcome to Horizon
installer training, Part 2.

2
00:00:05.323 --> 00:00:08.034
Plan the job. The math, and the limits.

3
00:00:08.484 --> 00:00:11.644
By the end, you will be
able to work out the lights,

4
00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:15.236
circuits, watts and amps for
a job, right at the quote.

5
00:00:15.686 --> 00:00:19.471
You will also know the four limits
every circuit must stay inside,

6
00:00:19.471 --> 00:00:21.671
and why we count lights, not feet.

7
00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:25.548
Volts, amps and watts always go together.

8
00:00:25.998 --> 00:00:28.663
If you know two of them,
you can find the third.

9
00:00:29.113 --> 00:00:34.889
Watts equals volts times amps. 24
volts times 4 amps is 96 watts.

10
00:00:35.339 --> 00:00:41.534
Amps equals watts divided by volts. 96
watts divided by 24 volts is 4 amps.

11
00:00:41.984 --> 00:00:43.964
This is the one you will use most.

12
00:00:44.414 --> 00:00:50.701
Volts equals watts divided by amps. 96
watts divided by 4 amps is 24 volts.

13
00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:54.872
Here is an easy way to remember it.
Cover the one you want with your thumb.

14
00:00:55.322 --> 00:00:57.383
What is left tells you what to do.

15
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:01.549
Now, planning a job. There are four steps.

16
00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:04.841
Do this at the quote, before
anybody climbs a ladder.

17
00:01:04.841 --> 00:01:06.754
All you need is the total feet.

18
00:01:07.204 --> 00:01:11.680
Step 1. How many lights?
Take the feet, times 1.5.

19
00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:17.269
Step 2. How many circuits? Take the
lights, divided by 60, and round up.

20
00:01:17.719 --> 00:01:23.589
Step 3. Watts per circuit. Take the
lights on that circuit, times 1.37.

21
00:01:24.039 --> 00:01:28.410
Step 4. Amps per circuit.
Take the watts, divided by 24.

22
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.719
The answer must be 4.0 amps or
less. If it is more, add a circuit.

23
00:01:35.069 --> 00:01:37.072
So where do those numbers come from?

24
00:01:37.522 --> 00:01:41.267
1.5 is lights per foot,
at our 8 inch spacing.

25
00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:46.483
1.37 is what one light really
pulls, in watts, on Pure White.

26
00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:49.378
And 24 is our system voltage.

27
00:01:50.728 --> 00:01:54.716
Let's work one. A house
with 150 feet of roofline.

28
00:01:55.166 --> 00:02:00.919
Step 1. 150 feet times 1.5 is 225 lights.

29
00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:08.875
Step 2. 225 divided by 60 is 3.75.
Round up, and that is 4 circuits.

30
00:02:09.325 --> 00:02:18.817
Step 3. 225 lights over 4 circuits is 56
lights each. 56 times 1.37 is 77 watts.

31
00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:24.289
Step 4. 77 watts divided
by 24 is 3.2 amps.

32
00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:28.994
3.2 amps is under 4.0. This plan passes.

33
00:02:30.344 --> 00:02:32.742
Now the same house, done the old way.

34
00:02:33.192 --> 00:02:39.316
Put 90 lights on one circuit.
90 times 1.37 is 123 watts.

35
00:02:39.766 --> 00:02:43.790
123 divided by 24 is 5.1 amps.

36
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.450
That is over the limit.
This is how wire gets hot.

37
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.771
So when the answer comes out over 4.0
amps, do not leave it. Add a circuit.

38
00:02:54.121 --> 00:02:57.715
One more check, before you count
circuits. Check the controller.

39
00:02:58.165 --> 00:02:59.675
The board has 6 circuits.

40
00:02:59.675 --> 00:03:04.336
How many are left for injection depends
on which controller the job uses.

41
00:03:04.786 --> 00:03:09.448
A single output controller uses 1
circuit. That leaves 5 for injection.

42
00:03:09.898 --> 00:03:14.316
A controller with 4 outputs uses
5 circuits. That leaves just 1.

43
00:03:14.766 --> 00:03:18.127
So find out which controller
the job uses, first.

44
00:03:19.477 --> 00:03:21.596
So how far does one box go?

45
00:03:22.046 --> 00:03:26.023
Five circuits covers about
300 lights, or 200 feet.

46
00:03:26.473 --> 00:03:31.750
A 400 foot house is 600 lights and
10 circuits. That is two boxes.

47
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.933
On a job with a 4 output
controller, you run out far sooner.

48
00:03:36.383 --> 00:03:38.711
Work it out at the quote, not on the roof.

49
00:03:40.047 --> 00:03:42.654
Here are the four limits
for every light circuit.

50
00:03:43.104 --> 00:03:46.082
60 lights. That is what
we aim for on a circuit.

51
00:03:46.532 --> 00:03:49.766
70 lights. That is the
most. Never go past it.

52
00:03:50.216 --> 00:03:53.403
4.0 amps. That is the most on a circuit.

53
00:03:53.853 --> 00:03:57.435
And 96 watts. That is also
the most on a circuit.

54
00:03:57.885 --> 00:04:03.533
Remember the math. 24 volts times 4
amps is 96 watts. The limits line up.

55
00:04:05.035 --> 00:04:08.814
Now, the biggest change. Count
lights. Never count feet.

56
00:04:09.264 --> 00:04:13.462
The old rule said, put an
injection every 60 to 70 feet.

57
00:04:13.912 --> 00:04:16.600
That rule is why our
circuits got overloaded.

58
00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:24.057
At 8 inch spacing, 60 to 70 feet is 90
to 105 lights. That is way over 4 amps.

59
00:04:24.507 --> 00:04:26.521
So stop using the old rule.

60
00:04:26.971 --> 00:04:29.488
The new rule is, inject every 60 lights.

61
00:04:29.488 --> 00:04:32.318
Never more than 70 lights on one circuit.

62
00:04:32.768 --> 00:04:37.604
At 8 inch spacing, that is 40
feet, and never past 47 feet.

63
00:04:38.954 --> 00:04:42.292
Here is what each circuit
pulls, at 24 volts.

64
00:04:42.742 --> 00:04:49.517
50 lights pull 69 watts, or 2.86 amps.
That is about 33 feet, and it is good.

65
00:04:49.967 --> 00:04:56.440
60 lights pull 82 watts, or 3.43
amps. That is about 40 feet. Aim here.

66
00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:02.736
65 lights pull 89 watts, or
3.71 amps. That is tight.

67
00:05:03.186 --> 00:05:08.812
70 lights pull 96 watts, or
4.00 amps. That is the most.

68
00:05:09.262 --> 00:05:14.102
80 lights is 4.57 amps.
90 lights is 5.14 amps.

69
00:05:14.102 --> 00:05:19.914
105 lights is 6.00 amps.
Every one of those is a stop.

70
00:05:20.364 --> 00:05:22.976
Feet in this table means 8 inch spacing.

71
00:05:22.976 --> 00:05:25.839
If the spacing changes, the feet change.

72
00:05:26.289 --> 00:05:29.615
The light count never changes.
That is why we count lights.

73
00:05:30.965 --> 00:05:32.771
Now, the Pure White trap.

74
00:05:33.221 --> 00:05:37.024
The sticker says one
light uses 0.96 watts.

75
00:05:37.474 --> 00:05:42.867
On PW, it really pulls 1.37
watts. That is 43% more.

76
00:05:43.317 --> 00:05:45.933
On Pure White, all three
colours turn on at once.

77
00:05:45.933 --> 00:05:49.174
That is why it pulls so much
more than the sticker says.

78
00:05:49.624 --> 00:05:52.672
Trust the sticker, and
you think 100 lights fit.

79
00:05:53.122 --> 00:05:55.927
Test it, and you hit 4 amps at 70.

80
00:05:56.377 --> 00:05:58.682
That gap is why breakers were tripping.

81
00:06:00.032 --> 00:06:02.894
So, always test on PW.

82
00:06:03.344 --> 00:06:07.936
A run can be fine on red or blue,
and still overload on white.

83
00:06:08.386 --> 00:06:13.036
Every amp test is done on PW. That
is the far-left white in the app.

84
00:06:13.486 --> 00:06:17.451
Not BW. BW has blue in it, and pulls less.

85
00:06:18.801 --> 00:06:21.710
Last, the plan sheet.
Fill it in at the quote.

86
00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.476
Use one row for each run on the house.

87
00:06:24.476 --> 00:06:27.773
Work left to right, using
the four planning steps.

88
00:06:28.201 --> 00:06:32.348
Feet. Then lights, which is
feet times 1.5. Then circuits,

89
00:06:32.348 --> 00:06:35.602
which is lights divided by 60, rounded up.

90
00:06:36.052 --> 00:06:39.082
Then lights each, which
must be 70 or less.

91
00:06:39.082 --> 00:06:42.293
Then amps each, which must be 4.0 or less.

92
00:06:42.743 --> 00:06:47.421
Here is the 150 foot house
from before. 225 lights,

93
00:06:47.421 --> 00:06:52.350
4 circuits, 56 lights each,
and 3.2 amps. It is OK.

94
00:06:53.056 --> 00:06:57.015
Then add up the total. If the job
needs more than 5 light circuits,

95
00:06:57.015 --> 00:06:58.565
it needs a second box.

96
00:06:59.933 --> 00:07:01.077
Let's lock it in.

97
00:07:01.527 --> 00:07:05.690
Amps equals watts divided by volts.
That is the one you use most.

98
00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:11.707
Lights are feet times 1.5. Circuits
are lights divided by 60, rounded up.

99
00:07:12.157 --> 00:07:17.074
Watts are lights times 1.37.
Amps are watts divided by 24.

100
00:07:17.524 --> 00:07:21.409
Aim for 60 lights on a
circuit. Never more than 70.

101
00:07:21.409 --> 00:07:26.610
4.0 amps and 96 watts are the
most. Over 4.0 amps? Add a circuit.

102
00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:32.481
Count lights, never feet. On Pure
White, one light pulls 1.37 watts,

103
00:07:32.481 --> 00:07:36.319
43% more than the sticker.
Always test on PW.

104
00:07:36.769 --> 00:07:40.779
Check the controller first, and
fill in the plan sheet at the quote.

105
00:07:40.779 --> 00:07:43.741
More than 5 light circuits
means a second box.

106
00:07:44.191 --> 00:07:49.689
Next, take the Part 2 quiz. Then in Part
3, we look at wire, parts and water.
